Article 4: When the People's Court determines whether the subject matter of insurance constitutes a "significant increase in the degree of danger" as prescribed in Articles 49 and 52 of the Insurance Law, it shall comprehensively consider the following factors:
(1) the change in the use of the subject matter of insurance;
(2) the change in the scope of use of the subject matter of insurance;
(3) the change in the environment where the subject matter of insurance is located;
(4) the change in the subject matter of insurance caused by refitting or other reasons;
(5) the change in the user or manager of the subject matter of insurance;
(6) the duration of the increase in the degree of danger;
(7) other factors that may lead to a significant increase in the degree of danger.
Although the degree of danger of the subject matter of insurance has increased, if the increased danger falls within the coverage scope of the insurance contract that the insurer foresaw or should have foreseen at the time of conclusion of the insurance contract, it does not constitute a significant increase in the degree of danger.

Article 14: Under any of the following circumstances, the insured may, in accordance with Article 65, Paragraph 2 of the Insurance Law, request the insurer to directly pay insurance money to the third party:
(1) the compensation liability borne by the insured to the third party has been confirmed by an effective judgment of the People's Court or an arbitral award;
(2) the compensation liability borne by the insured to the third party has been unanimously agreed upon between the insured and the third party;
(3) other circumstances under which the compensation liability that the insured should bear to the third party can be determined.
Under the circumstances provided in the preceding paragraph, if the insurer claims to determine the insurance compensation liability in accordance with the insurance contract, the People's Court shall support the claim.
